@article{geissler2008,
    title = {mTOR, Cancer and Transplantation},
    author = {Edward K. Geissler and Hans J. Schlitt and George Thomas},
    year = {2008},
    doi = {10.1111/j.1600-6143.2008.02391.x},
    abstract = {One of the most clinically important molecular signalling networks to emerge over the past decade is the mammalian target of rapamycin (mTOR) pathway. mTOR, the protein kinase at the core of this intricate and continually evolving pathway, controls cellular growth and behavior, impacting vital processes from immune reactivity to cancer progression. As researchers, surgeons and physicians in the field of organ transplantation, we have acquired a keen interest in regulating mTOR activity, because this molecule is not only able to block IL-2 signalling in T cells, and thus alloimmune reactivity, it is a critical part of the cellular circuitry which is often constitutively activated in neoplastic cells, leading to the all-too-often occurrence of cancer. Since allograft rejection and the development of cancer lead most lists for causing excess morbidity and mortality in our organ transplant population, a thorough and current understanding of the mTOR pathway becomes essential. In this review, we endeavor to unravel the latest molecular developments in mTOR signalling and use this basic knowledge to generate perspectives on how pharmacologic mTOR intervention may form a balance to impact long-term antidonor immune responses and the development of malignancy in transplant recipients. One of the most clinically important molecular signalling networks to emerge over the past decade is the mammalian target of rapamycin (mTOR) pathway. mTOR, the protein kinase at the core of this intricate and continually evolving pathway, controls cellular growth and behavior, impacting vital processes from immune reactivity to cancer progression. As researchers, surgeons and physicians in the field of organ transplantation, we have acquired a keen interest in regulating mTOR activity, because this molecule is not only able to block IL-2 signalling in T cells, and thus alloimmune reactivity, it is a critical part of the cellular circuitry which is often constitutively activated in neoplastic cells, leading to the all-too-often occurrence of cancer. Since allograft rejection and the development of cancer lead most lists for causing excess morbidity and mortality in our organ transplant population, a thorough and current understanding of the mTOR pathway becomes essential. In this review, we endeavor to unravel the latest molecular developments in mTOR signalling and use this basic knowledge to generate perspectives on how pharmacologic mTOR intervention may form a balance to impact long-term antidonor immune responses and the development of malignancy in transplant recipients. The discovery of rapamycin from a soil sample collected on Easter Island in the 1970s has had a profound effect on the study of intracellular pathways that operate through mTOR. Indeed, mTOR is a central player for integrating different biochemical and growth factor signals, including amino acids, glucose, ATP and insulin (1Dann SG Selvaraj A Thomas G. mTOR Complex1-S6K1 signaling: At the crossroads of obesity, diabetes and cancer..Trends Mol Med. 2007; 13: 252-259Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (403) Google Scholar). It follows, therefore, that a molecule capable of sensing nutrients and the bioenergetic status of the cell is positioned advantageously to control how a cell should best coordinate signals simultaneously received from growth factors. Fundamental cellular processes directly affected by mTOR activation include protein translation, ribosome biosynthesis and cell cycle progression. With this level of control, mTOR can influence vital immunologic responses, such as lymphocyte proliferation, developmental processes, including neurologic and muscle generation, and the malignant growth of cancer cells. Consequently, it is no wonder why the discovery of rapamycin has generated so much interest in further dissecting the complexities of the mTOR pathway and applying mTOR inhibitors to treat conditions where cells proliferate to pathologic levels. In organ transplantation, perceptive pioneers thought to take advantage of these discoveries to use mTOR inhibitors as effective immunosuppressants for preventing allograft rejection and, most recently, we are presently testing their potential to simultaneously reduce the problem of cancer in transplant recipients, as well as in the general population. This review aims to give the latest perspectives on the mTOR pathway and to suggest how we might take optimal advantage of pharmacologic mTOR inhibition when dealing with cancer, particularly in a transplantation setting. The mTOR pathway involves an ever-expanding number of upstream and downstream integrative signalling molecules. Since it is not possible to detail all the various intricacies of this pathway in a concise review, we will rather attempt to form an image of the most essential, novel and clinically relevant elements (see Figure 1). One of the most interesting recent findings is that mTOR itself is part of two distinct signalling complexes; one complex, mTOR Complex1 (mTORC1) receives a signal from an upstream molecule AKT, and the second complex, mTOR Complex2 (mTORC2) regulates AKT through Ser473 phosphorylation (2Wullschleger S Loewith R Hall MN. TOR signaling in growth and metabolism..Cell. 2006; 124: 471-484Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (4741) Google Scholar). AKT is activated via phosphoinositide-dependent kinase-1 (PDK1) phosphorylation at a second site, Thr308, with phosphorylation at both sites required for maximal AKT activity. Continuing upstream of PDK1, binding of insulin-like growth factors to their cognate receptors (e.g. insulin receptor 1) results in recruitment of receptor substrates (e.g. insulin receptor substrate; IRS-1), and binding of phosphoinositide 3-kinase (PI3K). PI3K converts phosphatidylinositiol-4,5-phosphate (PIP2) to phosphatidylinositiol-3,4,5-phosphate (PIP3), which recruits PDK1 to AKT, thus potentiating the mTOR pathway. Importantly, PTEN (phosphatase and tensin homologue deleted on chromosome 10), a tumor suppressor, reverses the PIP2-to-PIP3 reaction, thus reducing AKT activity. When activated, AKT mediates mTORC1 activation via inhibition of a tumor suppressor complex made up the two tuberous sclerosis proteins, hamartin (TSC1) and tuberin (TSC2). The TSC1/2 complex functions as a GTPase-activating protein, resulting in inactivation of Rheb (Ras homolog enriched in brain). In the GTP-bound state, Rheb selectively complexes with and activates mTORC1. A notable additional activity of AKT is promotion of cell survival by phosphorylating caspase 9 and activating the Bcl-2 antagonist of cell death (BAD) (3Plas DR Thompson CB. AKT-dependent transformation: There is more to growth than just surviving..Oncogene. 2005; 24: 7435-7442Crossref PubMed Scopus (341) Google Scholar). From another perspective, Lee et al. (4Lee DF Kuo HP Chen CT et al.IKKβ suppression of TSC1 links inflammation and tumor angiogenesis via the mTOR pathway..Cell. 2007; 130: 440-455Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (542) Google Scholar) have recently discovered a novel molecular link from tumor necrosis factor α (TNFα)-associated chronic inflammation to mTOR. These authors show that inhibitory kappaB kinase beta (IKKβ), a kinase downstream of TNFα, integrates with mTORC1 by inhibiting TSC1/2, thus providing a new molecular link between inflammation and cancer. In general, TSC1/2 appears to be an important signalling hub for integration into the mTORC1 pathway. The primary downstream effectors of mTORC1 are S6 kinase 1 (S6K1) and eukaryotic initiation factor 4E (eIF-4E) binding protein-1 (4E-BP1), which are both important regulators of mRNA translation. mTORC1 activates S6K1, which in-turn phosphorylates a number of substrates including ribosomal protein S6, a protein thought to support the translation of 5′TOP mRNAs, thereby increasing cellular RNA by up to 20\%; more recent studies, however, challenge this supposition and suggest mTORC1 may actually control 5′TOP mRNA translation via a different mechanism (2Wullschleger S Loewith R Hall MN. TOR signaling in growth and metabolism..Cell. 2006; 124: 471-484Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (4741) Google Scholar). More recently additional S6K1 substrates have been identified, which are involved in controlling protein synthesis including translation initiation factor 4B, a helicase for 5′ ends of mRNA; eEF2 kinase, a mediator of the phosphorylation of translation elongation factor 2, involved in controlling protein synthesis transit rates; BAD1, a proapoptotic protein; SKAR, a nuclear protein proposed to couple transcription with pre-mRNA splicing; and most recently, programmed cell death protein 4 (PDCD4), an inhibitor of translation initiation factor eIF4A. mTORC1 also promotes cap-dependent translation by phosphorylating 4E-BP1, releasing eIF-4E, which promotes translation initiation. Other basic vital processes, such as ribosome biogenesis via RNA polymerases are coordinated by mTOR control of various related transcription factors. Even lipid metabolism is under the influence of mTORC1, since mTORC1 positively influences peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor γ (PPARγ), which has an important role in adipogenesis and lipid accumulation (5Kim JE Chen J. Regulation of peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor-γ activity by mammalian target of rapamycin and amino acids in adipogenesis..Diabetes. 2004; 53: 2748-2756Crossref PubMed Scopus (368) Google Scholar). Therefore, as a consequence of mTOR network dysfunction or pharmacologic intervention, various essential cell processes are affected, including development and function of the immune system, carcinogenesis and progression, angiogenesis and wound-healing, as well as development of obesity and diabetes. The discovery that molecules downstream of mTORC1 can negatively feedback signals to PI3K–AKT has brought new light and complexity into studying the overall effects of mTOR inhibitors. One such feedback loop involves S6K1. It has now been shown that S6K1 downregulates IRS-1, which forms part of the insulin receptor complex responsible for ‘upstream’ AKT Thr308-site phosphorylation (1Dann SG Selvaraj A Thomas G. mTOR Complex1-S6K1 signaling: At the crossroads of obesity, diabetes and cancer..Trends Mol Med. 2007; 13: 252-259Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (403) Google Scholar). Therefore, active mTORC1 induction of S6K1 unpredictably inhibits one of its primary upstream activators, AKT. Moreover, it appears that constitutive activation of mTORC1 via TSC1/2 deletions inhibits signalling by other similar growth factor receptors, such as platelet-derived growth factor receptor (PDGFR) (6Zhang H Bajraszewski N Wu E et al.PDGFRs are critical for PI3K/AKT activation and negatively regulated by mTOR..J Clin Invest. 2007; 117: 730-738Crossref PubMed Scopus (310) Google Scholar), indicating other receptor-tyrosine kinase pathways are also subject to mTORC1 effector molecule negative regulation. mTORC1 negative feedback is thought to be responsible for the relative nonaggressive nature of tumors associated with TSC1/2 mutations, restraining mTORC1 pathway overactivation. The diverse input and high level of regulatory complexity impacting the mTOR pathway, which can only be superficially discussed here, is an evolutionary indicator of its importance for the cell, and organism. An important distinction when studying mTOR and its pharmacologic inhibitors is its association with several other proteins, most critically, regulatory associated protein of mTOR (Raptor) and rapamycin insensitive companion of mTOR (Rictor). Knockout mice for Raptor and Rictor, as well as siRNA knockdown experiments, have helped to sort out their exclusive relationship between mTORC1 and mTORC2. These studies show that Raptor is indispensable for mTORC1 activation, and may help complex Rheb-GTP with mTOR. Most recently, however, a novel endogenous inhibitor of mTOR, FKBP38, has been shown to interact with Rheb, preventing its association with mTORC1; therefore, Rheb likely activates mTORC1 by antagonizing FKBP38 (7Bai X Ma D Liu A et al.Rheb activates mTOR by antagonizing its endogenous inhibitor, FKBP38..Science. 2007; 318: 977-980Crossref PubMed Scopus (313) Google Scholar). Regarding Rictor, it rather associates exclusively with mTORC2 and is tied to its activity since experimentally nullifying Rictor markedly reduces phosphorylation of Ser473 on AKT (8Hresko RC Mueckler M. mTOR·RICTOR is the Ser473 kinase for AKT/protein kinase B in 3T3-L1 adipocytes..J Biol Chem. 2005; 280: 40406-40416Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (513) Google Scholar). Although we have gained a basic understanding of the extracellular growth factors and stimuli that activate the PI3K-AKT-mTORC1 pathway, it remains uncertain what input triggers mTORC2. One significant aspect of two different mTOR complexes is that pharmacologic rapamycin analogs [e.g. sirolimus and CCI-779 (Wyeth), RAD001 (Novartis), AP23573 (Ariad Pharma); all of which will simply be referred to as ‘rapamycin’] are directly effective at blocking only mTORC1. Upon entering the cell, rapamycin binds to the cytoplasmic receptor protein, FKBP12, which is the same protein that FK506/tacrolimus associates with. The FKBP12-rapamycin complex only recognizes mTOR when it exists as part of mTORC1, resulting in inhibition of mTORC1 activity. It is also important to recognize that FKBP12-rapamycin forms a nearly irreversible interaction with mTORC1, leading to an inhibition period that extends beyond systemically circulating levels of drug. Moreover, studies suggest that rapamycin acts as a sink with regard to its high affinity and binding of newly formed mTOR, depriving mTOR from forming nascent mTORC2 complexes (9Sarbassov DD Ali SM Sengupta S et al.Prolonged rapamycin treatment inhibits mTORC2 assembly and AKT/PKB..Mol Cell. 2006; 22: 159-168Abstract Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus (2206) Google Scholar). Therefore, although rapamycin analogs only directly block mTORC1, they also have some capacity for indirectly inhibiting mTORC2 (Figure 1). It is tempting to speculate that the combination of rapamycin with a yet undeveloped pharmacologic mTORC2-specific inhibitor could be beneficial, especially in light of data indicating that abnormally high PI3K signalling in cancer cells may be particularly susceptible to mTORC2 inhibition (10Hietakangas V Cohen SM. Re-evaluating AKT regulation: Role of TOR complex 2 in tissue growth..Genes Dev. 2007; 21: 632-637Crossref PubMed Scopus (114) Google Scholar). A dual mTORC1/2 inhibitor treatment hypothesis is sustained by findings that rapamycin use alone disengages the mTORC1 negative feedback loop that normally inhibits PI3K-AKT overactivation. However, the toxicity with such a treatment will be a major question. While anticipating these pharmacologic advancements, a deeper understanding of the mTOR pathway intricacies will better to for and for cancer. The review thus has to the and complexities of the mTOR pathway. In the of the review, our is to this knowledge to in why rapamycin may be effective in at some forms of of which in organ transplant recipients. The PTEN is or in a of different The PTEN tumor suppressor protein is in a critical upstream of AKT and of its activity results in a of the AKT mTORC1 all the processes vital for tumor cell proliferation, survival and support (e.g. the that a of PTEN is to including A E et PTEN results in and Clin Invest. 2004; PubMed Scopus Google Scholar), it is for the with PTEN protein 2007; Full Text Full Text PDF PubMed Scopus Google Scholar). a recent study that pharmacologic mTORC1 inhibition tumor growth in mice with PTEN E E et pathway a critical role in the development of Med. 2007; 13: PubMed Scopus Google Scholar). downstream from the of AKT is in a of malignant to upstream signalling via PTEN mutations, by mutations, or by abnormally molecule